{"cells":[{"kind":1,"value":"# TEST WEEK 1","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 1. Write a function to return a list of the first “x” odd numbers. Example:\n```\nf[5]\n1 3 5 7 9","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"firstOddNumbers:    {[x]\n                        numList:    til (x*2);\n                        oddNums:    numList where 1=numList mod 2;\n                        :oddNums\n\n }\n\n\nfirstOddNumbers[5]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"1 3 5 7 9\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 2. Define the two following lists in your q session:\n```\nlist1:  (`a;`b;`c;1;2;3)\nlist2:  (`d;`b;`c;1;2;4)","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"list1:  (`a;`b;`c;1;2;3);\nlist2:  (`d;`b;`c;1;2;4);","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### a. return the list of elements common to both list 1 and list 2","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"list1 inter list2","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"`b\r\n`c\r\n1\r\n2\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### b. Return all elements which occur in either list1 or list2","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"(list1 except list2), (list2 except list1)","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"`a\r\n3\r\n`d\r\n4\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 3. Return a list of all numbers between 0 and 1000 that are divisible by 6.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"listNum:    0,(1 + til 1000);\nlistNum where 0=listNum mod 6","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"0 6 12 18 24 30 36 42 48 54 60 66 72 78 84 90 96 102 108 114 120 126 132 138 ..\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":2,"value":"last listNum","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"1000\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 4. Write a function which calculates the “score” of a string. The “score” will be calculated as follows:\n```\nLower case characters are scored according to position in the alphabet, ie.: a=1 points, b=2 points, c=3 points …\nUpper case characters are worth twice as many as lower case: A=2 points, B=4 points, C=6 points …\n\nf[\"Test\"]\n\n84","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"scoreCalc:  {[x]\n                lowercase:  .Q.a;\n                score:      sum {$[x in lowercase; 1+lowercase?x;2*(1+ lowercase?(lower x))]} each x;\n                :score\n                };\n\n\nscoreCalc[\"Test\"]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"84\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 5. Write a function which takes a list of integers as an argument and applies the following conditions to each element:\n```\n· If the number is odd, add 3\n· If the number is a multiple of 4, multiply the number by 4\n· If none of the conditions are met, set the value to 0\n\nf[1 2 3 4]\n4 0 6 16\n","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"threeOrFour:    {{[x] $[1= x mod 2; x+3; 0 = x mod 4; x*4; 0]} each  x};\n\nthreeOrFour[1 2 3 4]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"4 0 6 16\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 6. What is the maximum number of arguments a kdb+ function can have? What would be some ways you could get around this limit?","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"```\n8 formal parameters;\n\nwe get around this by passing in a dictionary","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]}]}